"Anesidora Island is a man-made hero zone at Point Nemo, where registered superhumans live."
"The Allied Heroes of Earth own Anesidora, along with other Avowed zones around the world."
"On registration an Avowed's citizenship moves automatically to Anesidora."
"Anesidora is hard to travel to; visitors arrive by System teleport at a travel center."
"Day passes for visitors are only for special circumstances, bigwigs and immediate family."
"The island has no tourist industry, and 'Anesidora for Anesidorans' types resent outsiders."
"Anesidora has two parts: crescent-shaped Apex in the north and round, larger F City in the south."
"The Span, an eight-kilometer bridge, joins Apex to F City."
"Anesidora has warm summers, a new ice park and martial arts showcases."
"The island grows no produce; bananas are costly and customs bars taking fresh ones off it."
"The island government pays for official teleports and runs the NesiCard debit card system."
"Minors, non-Avowed residents and visitors pay with a NesiCard issued at help desks by thumbprint."
"Anesidora is governed by the Anesidoran High Council, which has a President."
"A Rabbit sits on the High Council; Aulia Velra once ran for its President and lost badly."
"The Velras, a Chainer-hoarding family, are among Anesidora's most powerful families."
"Island schools use placement exams; outside transcripts don't transfer and school is paid for."
"New students must live in the dorms for their first couple of years."
"Teens new to the island live in intake dorms if they don't go straight into a school."
"Advanced training programs require fluency in one of Earth's five most-spoken languages."
"Classes on safe contracting are standard at Anesidoran high schools."
"Earth's yearly one or two demons land in a controlled zone on Anesidora."
"A few hundred Avowed destroy each demon in seconds, and no media are allowed to watch."
"Formal strike crews and elite battle squads based on Anesidora handle major emergencies."
"Island-born Avowed children prep for their classes for years before selection."
"Anesidorans rarely get along with Avowed who refuse to live on the island."
"Some outsiders call it a 'fancy prison island'; one Australian Avowed calls it 'Aneshitora'."
"The island has a main hospital and a couple of Healers who do full rejuvenation."
"Post Drop mailboxes, talking Wright-made devices, carry island mail for half an Argold."
"D- and F-rank Avowed often spend their lives on the island or in another Avowed zone."
"The island is wholly artificial, with no natural boulders and few birds but park ducks and geese."
"Anesidora faces the open Pacific, with nothing between its south shore and Antarctica."
"Basic healthcare is free, and education is free and mandatory in some form until age 21."
"No one is homeless; anyone who can't afford a home after school is given a small apartment."
"The interface gives free internet on the island, and ads come on paper, never as System spam."
"An Avowed can vote the moment their feet touch Anesidoran ground."
"Speech is free, but protests and large public gatherings need permits."
"Using powers is legal unless it hurts, inconveniences or destroys; the strong get less leeway."
"In family-friendly districts, offensive power use can bring a decade or more in jail."
"Gambling and many genetic modifications are legal; designer babies are common."
"Alcohol is very illegal, after drunk Avowed knocked the tops off skyscrapers."
"Returning Avowed must hand over wizard pills, shots and potions, and are reimbursed."
"New Avowed receive a monthly Argold stipend for their first year and a half on the island."
"Native Anesidorans often speak Mandarin with loan words."
"Evac Priority cards on the interface rank who is rescued first in Apex."
"The island is under a century old, in the South Pacific some four kilometers above the seabed."
"A large share of Earth's Avowed live there; raising children on the island is basically free."
"Islanders mostly ride public transit, then mopeds and motorbikes; cars carry a heavy import tax."
"'North' and crescents signal eliteness on Anesidora."
"Signs carry Artonan logograms so every language group can read them."
"Superhuman path etiquette puts super speed on the far left, and the faster avoid the slower."
"Island-born Avowed call newcomers 'globies'."
"Anesidora has hardly any pests and no mosquitoes."
"Earth's yearly demon appears in an offshore facility, where a select battle group destroys it."
"The island is not private: Sways, audials and listening gear and spells are everywhere."
"The Informant, an old Wright, sells cheap spy wearables that record public spaces."
"Unregistered Avowed who come in willingly before twenty are treated as legitimate."
"Older or caught unregistered Avowed serve two logged probationary years before citizenship."
"Harboring an unregistered Avowed can get a student expelled."
"Visitors can be scheduled two years ahead; holiday slots fill the moment they open."
"The island celebrates holidays lavishly, with a whole Halloweek before Halloween."
"Superhuman sporting events and performances sell out in seconds."
"The Anesidoran Avowed Ballet mixes ballet with acrobatics and spells, and tours Earth."
"The High Council's Unified Rares and Uniques seat speaks for classes too small for their own."
"Aulia Velra held the Unified Rares and Uniques seat for decades before losing it."
"Anesidora was made because ordinary humans wanted to limit Avowed access to the rest of Earth."
"The Nautilus Needle is the tallest skyscraper on Anesidora."
"Attacking with powers in a safe family zone brings an on-call hero, a fine and a public ban."
"Islanders buy lavish 'Shit Sixteen' gifts for children whose selection comes out disappointing."
"Island hospitals combine doctors, Healers and a couple of wizards; potions replace aspirin."
"Healers treat injured Avowed first; criminals get human surgeons."
"Native islanders are fascinated by wild animals, and F City has a small zoo."
"The Anesidoran government and resident Avowed may use the System's personal teleport."
